WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.820
An international tribunal has ruled that India should not have suspended a 65-year-old water-sharing treaty with Pakistan, raising stakes for 300 million people who rely on the rivers. This is Omni View, episode 174. New Delhi had halted the Indus Waters Treaty last year after blaming Islamabad for a militant attack on tourists in Kashmir. The South China Morning Post frames the outcome as a legal rebuke that hands Pakistan a diplomatic win.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:51.900
At a moment when both countries already face water stress, Indian officials have long argued that the halt was a legitimate response to security threats. In their account, river cooperation cannot be treated as a separate file after an attack on civilians they tie to Pakistan, and suspension was a lawful signal rather than a casual walk away from a contract.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:01:11.240
Pakistani representatives see the decision as confirmation that the treaty cannot be set aside by one government acting alone. In that telling, the pact is not a favor to be switched off in a crisis, and an independent ruling against unilateral suspension is simply the legal text doing what it always said it would do.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:30.480
The rivers in question sustain agriculture and cities across the subcontinent, which is why a fight over the treaty is also a fight over farms, drinking water, and urban supply. More than 1,400 people crossed from Yemen into Djibouti in a single day as fighting intensified in Western Yemen.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:39.360
Al Jazeera reports the rapid movement of civilians toward the Bob Al-Mandeb Strait, the narrow waterway at the southern end of the Red Sea.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:52.840
Houthi rebels said they struck a Saudi military base. The surge follows weeks of clashes that have already killed hundreds and displaced tens of thousands. Djibouti authorities recorded the arrivals at the port of Obok.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:01.720
Yemeni government sources described the fighting as an attempt to regain territory. Houthi statements link their actions to broader regional pressure.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:09.460
The new crossings add to earlier flows caused by the same round of clashes, and Djibouti is the authority counting who arrives.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:24.620
United Nations Secretary General Antonio Guteraz told reporters in Delhi that deadlock in the Security Council, the United Nations' body that can authorize international action, does not mean the wider United Nations has failed.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:30.080
Everything in that argument turns on whether a frozen council equals a broken organization.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:43.420
The Hindu reports Guteraz drawing a line between the Vito-bound Security Council, where a single permanent member can block a resolution, and the rest of the UN system that still does technical and humanitarian work.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:55.780
He spoke while attending the BRICS summit, a gathering of major emerging economies, praised India's mediation efforts between Russia and Ukraine, and addressed questions about a map shown during his visit.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:02.500
Some member states have argued that repeated Vito's have left the council unable to act on major conflicts.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:14.820
Guteraz pointed to UN agencies still delivering aid and monitoring as evidence the organization remains functional, in a period when the council has been unable to pass resolutions on several active wars.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:24.940
France's UN mission withdrew a social media post that had criticized how the United States voted at the General Assembly, the forum where all member states cast votes.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:32.880
The original post had highlighted differing positions on a recent resolution. US officials said they welcomed the retraction.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:41.960
French diplomats described the post as an unintended escalation, and the apology was published on Friday after the original message drew criticism.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.260
The two countries continued to cooperate on many files despite the momentary friction.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:54.020
Japan's trade minister said future Japanese investment in the United States will prioritize the power sector.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:04:06.340
Nikkeh Asia reports the minister linking that shift to rising electricity demand from data centers, the warehouses of servers behind cloud computing and artificial intelligence, and from manufacturing.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.700
The comments came during discussions on bilateral economic ties.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:21.440
Japanese firms see stable returns in power projects that align with both countries supply chain goals, and US officials have welcomed additional capital in energy infrastructure

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:35.240
as talks on expanding investment flows continue. Fujitsu announced plans to sell artificial intelligence or AI, chips developed from its supercomputer technology to markets in the United States and Asia.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:43.560
Nikkeh Asia describes the move as part of Japan's effort to expand its role in advanced semiconductors beyond domestic customers.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:51.060
The chips are intended for data center and research use, and the designs have already been proven in high performance computing.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:05:00.020
US and Asian buyers are seeking alternatives to existing suppliers, and the export plans target both established and emerging markets.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.140
Progress worth knowing on student finance in the United Kingdom.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:10.180
The government said it will update guidance, so borrowers understand that repayment terms can change.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:18.060
The Guardian reports the change as a direct response to findings that some promotional material had understated flexibility in the rules.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:31.520
A parliamentary report had criticized earlier materials for not highlighting that possibility, and the concerns were raised in a July report by members of parliament, or MPs, about how loan conditions were presented.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:39.000
The new wording will be added to official documents before the next academic year, and student groups have welcomed the clarification.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:53.000
To really understand how a longstanding water treaty is supposed to work when politics collapse, you have to look at the technical machinery built into the 1960 text, not at the on-off language of a press statement.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:58.320
A common misunderstanding treats suspension as a simple switch that ends the whole bargain.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:08.740
The Indus Waters Treaty instead created a permanent commission and a multi-step dispute process that was designed to keep running even when political relations break down.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:18.800
That commission has met regularly since 1960, exchanging data on river flows and resolving technical questions without needing a green light from either capital.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:26.180
The 1960 text created the commission precisely to keep technical cooperation separate from wider political disputes.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:37.100
When one side stops cooperating, the treaty's arbitration clause still lets the other party take the matter to an independent tribunal, whose ruling is binding on the technical provisions.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:44.980
The recent Hague decision addressed only the legality of suspension and left the underlying allocation of river water unchanged.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.900
Historical records show the commission continued functioning through earlier wars and diplomatic breaks.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:59.420
The design is a form of insulation against political shocks, using a permanent technical body rather than hoping goodwill survives a crisis.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:13.280
When you next hear that a treaty has been ended, check whether the commission or the arbitration process is still operating, and read what the actual text says about unilateral withdrawal rather than relying on a summary of a speech.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:20.760
If you want to go deeper on this week's regional diplomacy, compare how the South China Morning Post and the Hindu are covering it.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.140
You will see two different frames on the same set of facts.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:29.700
Looking back across the week just gone, here is a short catch-up on threads you may have missed.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:38.080
The United States and China explored direct talks on artificial intelligence safety rules, even as their technology competition intensified.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:48.540
China's exports rose 25% last month, pushing its annual trade surplus past $800 billion and shifting pressure onto global supply chains.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:56.340
Oil crossing $100 a barrel raised fuel and goods costs for households and businesses across import dependent economies.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:03.420
Tomorrow, watch for whether India or Pakistan issues a formal reply to the tribunal on the Indus Waters Treaty.

